{
	title: 'Web tab bar and list sheet: card-tinted header, rail and status dots',
	spec: "At all viewport widths, the web tab bar is a two-tier console: a card-tinted title header (3px left project LED, dark project tint, project-colored number, bright readable name and status indicator) beside the new-tab button (fine pointers) and the list/count button on row 1, over a full-width natively scrolling number rail on row 2. Desktop (> 40rem) uses a 40px header, a 32px rail, dot-plus-label status text, and a larger burger-and-count button (18px bars, 14px numerals). There is no top-bar Notify button on desktop or mobile (Notifications lives in the tab list sheet and session menu). Phones (<= 40rem) use compact header padding and count sizing (13px bars, 12px numerals) and compact status marks (blinking dot while working, state word while waiting), with touch targets at least 44px on coarse pointers. Rail tabs show neutral numbers, a bottom project LED bar (2px inset, 3px full-width when active), and a separate 6px round status dot in the top-right corner colored from the host theme's [data-mark] rules: solid while waiting, hollow while paused, smoothly pulsing in theme colors (including jobs color) while working. No solid neon fills, rings over numbers, or blinking squares. The count button opens the tab list sheet and shows an inline dot for the most urgent waiting mark among tabs under 10% visible. Selecting a tab centers it on the nearest tab start; scrolling never selects. The tab list sheet has a compact header with total count and close button, rows with a 3px left project LED, aligned numbers, a dedicated status-dot column, project-tinted active row (never status green), a 2x2 primary action grid (New tab, Find, Notifications, Reload page), and a compact utility row for diagnostics and touch debug. Replaces task ce's chevrons, task 3k's web menu button and equal web cells, task gn's tinted current cell and task fr's web glyphs; the terminal is unchanged.",
